                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Khazaria",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "87558583-ea03-3b50-af57-e8c96eef59bf",
                                        "entry_id": "166a8d74-54e5-369c-bf25-c7ef6d77bfb0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A polity, established in medieval Eurasia by Khazars, that occupied much of modern-day European Russia, western Kazakhstan, eastern Ukraine, Azerbaijan, large portions of the northern Caucasus, parts of Georgia, the Crimea, and northeastern Turkey."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3e7f3e7b-6e01-4f52-8858-2a59216cb6ab",
                                                "sense_id": "87558583-ea03-3b50-af57-e8c96eef59bf",
                                                "text": "2007, Vladimir Ja. Petrukhin, Khazaria and Rus': An Examination of Their Historical Relations, Peter B. Golden, Haggai Ben-Shammai, András Róna-Tas (editors), The World of the Khazars: New Perspectives, Brill Publishers, page 245,\nAn impartial evaluation of the relations between Khazaria and Rus' (or Eastern Slavs) within the limits imposed by official Soviet historiography was rather uncommon: the role of Khazaria was mainly pictured as an obstacle in the highly progressive processes of the development of the Russian state."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bdde8eef-0e5e-41af-9ef3-569c28e4e438",
                                                "sense_id": "87558583-ea03-3b50-af57-e8c96eef59bf",
                                                "text": "Jews continued to migrate to Khazaria from the Muslim and Byzantine lands circa 943 because they were still being forcefully converted to Christianity by Romanus, according to al-Masudi."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a6621118-e3ff-46a7-94b0-a4b86d07597e",
                                                "sense_id": "87558583-ea03-3b50-af57-e8c96eef59bf",
                                                "text": "In 860, Byzantine Emperor Michael III Amorian and the Ecumenical Patriarch and Constantinopolitan Bishop Photios I who was Cyril's teacher dispatched the brothers on a missionary expedition to Khazaria, the Turkic Khazar Khaganate north of Persia to prevent the expansion of Judaism there."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
